  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
